WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.315 --> 00:00:07.359
So it's my pleasure to
introduce Daniel MacArthur,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.744
who is from just down the street
and has been instrumental in

00:00:10.745 --> 00:00:13.181
creating a extremely
valuable resource,

00:00:13.182 --> 00:00:16.697
that based on the title of his
talk, I think he's going to tell

00:00:16.698 --> 00:00:20.305
us about and probably how it's
changing the world, so great.

00:00:20.306 --> 00:00:21.495
&gt;&gt; Cool, thank you Jennifer and

00:00:21.496 --> 00:00:24.114
thanks for the opportunity to be
here today it's a great pleasure

00:00:24.115 --> 00:00:25.079
to be here, of course.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.181
So Jennifer mentioned I
work just down the road at

00:00:27.182 --> 00:00:28.163
the Broad Institute.

00:00:28.164 --> 00:00:28.882
I have a lab, but

00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:31.511
I also spend a fair bit of time
over at Mass General Hospital.

00:00:31.512 --> 00:00:34.138
We have two focuses, one is on
rare disease diagnosis, which

00:00:34.139 --> 00:00:36.779
you've heard a lot about and
I'm not gonna talk about today.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.586
And the second,
as Jennifer mentioned,

00:00:38.587 --> 00:00:41.010
is thinking about ways in which
we can build up very large

00:00:41.011 --> 00:00:44.250
collections of genetic variation
from the general population.

00:00:44.251 --> 00:00:45.720
Primarily, with
the focus of using

00:00:45.721 --> 00:00:49.130
these as reference databases to
understand what variants do and

00:00:49.131 --> 00:00:51.880
which ones are most important
for causation of rare disease.

00:00:53.112 --> 00:00:55.690
And I think I wanted to
just start with generally

00:00:55.691 --> 00:00:57.820
framing a sense of why I'm so

00:00:57.821 --> 00:01:00.010
excited about human genetics
as we stand right now.

00:01:00.011 --> 00:01:02.570
I think it's actually the most
exciting field that's

00:01:02.571 --> 00:01:03.490
going on at the moment.

00:01:03.491 --> 00:01:06.142
And it's the most exciting time
to be working in human genetics.

00:01:06.143 --> 00:01:08.653
And that's because human
genetics provides us a way of

00:01:08.654 --> 00:01:11.056
moving back and forth between
two very different and

00:01:11.057 --> 00:01:12.595
powerful kinds of information.

00:01:12.596 --> 00:01:14.034
So we are becoming
increasingly good,

00:01:14.035 --> 00:01:15.691
as you've heard from
other speakers today,

00:01:15.692 --> 00:01:17.940
at layering information
across the genome.

00:01:17.941 --> 00:01:20.590
So here in this random
screenshot from

00:01:20.591 --> 00:01:23.730
the USOC Genome Browser, we
have some chunk of the genome.

00:01:23.731 --> 00:01:25.330
And for
each base in the human genome,

00:01:25.331 --> 00:01:30.110
we have information about if
that's present in a gene or not.

00:01:30.111 --> 00:01:32.083
If so, where that gene
is actually expressed.

00:01:32.084 --> 00:01:33.493
If it's in a non coding region,

00:01:33.494 --> 00:01:35.845
whether that is also a
regulatory region that may play

00:01:35.846 --> 00:01:37.736
some role in regulating
gene expression.

00:01:37.737 --> 00:01:40.508
And this information is
increasing thanks to information

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:42.825
like ENCODE and
Epigenomics Roadmap and GTEx and

00:01:42.826 --> 00:01:43.740
other resources.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.625
And then another type of
information that we as

00:01:47.626 --> 00:01:50.400
the broader biomedical community
have become very good at

00:01:50.401 --> 00:01:52.930
collecting is information
about human phenotypes.

00:01:52.931 --> 00:01:55.720
We now have millions of people
around the world who have been

00:01:55.721 --> 00:01:58.970
collected in large,
often national scale bio banks.

00:01:58.971 --> 00:02:00.860
And these people have
a whole variety of different

00:02:00.861 --> 00:02:02.300
bits of information
collected about them.

00:02:02.301 --> 00:02:04.755
And these can range from
information about particular

00:02:04.756 --> 00:02:06.621
diseases they've
been diagnosed with,

00:02:06.622 --> 00:02:09.350
which may be stored in a more or
less well formatted fashion.

00:02:09.351 --> 00:02:11.304
So all sorts of electronic
medical record data,

00:02:11.305 --> 00:02:12.402
prescription registers,

00:02:12.403 --> 00:02:14.320
quantitative trade data and
questionnaires.

00:02:14.321 --> 00:02:18.550
And then in many cases, a whole
bunch of interesting and rich

00:02:18.551 --> 00:02:21.060
biological or functional genomic
data, like transcript mix or

00:02:21.061 --> 00:02:23.890
metabeilomix that can give us
some insight into the biology of

00:02:23.891 --> 00:02:25.680
those individuals.

00:02:25.681 --> 00:02:26.890
And human genetics is cool,

00:02:26.891 --> 00:02:29.370
in part, because it provides us
with a way of moving back and

00:02:29.371 --> 00:02:31.520
forth between these two
kinds of information.

00:02:31.521 --> 00:02:32.516
So the traditional,

00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:34.730
forward genetics way of
doing human genetics,

00:02:34.731 --> 00:02:37.456
is that we would collect people
on the bases of phenotype.

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.400
So do something like what
Gill just talked about,

00:02:39.401 --> 00:02:41.626
identify certain families who
have some interesting and

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.294
very rare disease,
group those people and

00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:45.170
then see what pieces of
DNA they have in common.

00:02:45.171 --> 00:02:48.290
That is what variation they have
in common, and use that to zoom

00:02:48.291 --> 00:02:50.740
in on particular genes that
may underlay their disease.

00:02:50.741 --> 00:02:52.260
This has been an incredibly
productive approach.

00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:54.750
We now know more than 4,000
genes associated with

00:02:54.751 --> 00:02:55.820
Mendelian diseases.

00:02:55.821 --> 00:02:58.497
And many thousands of regions in
the genome that are associated

00:02:58.498 --> 00:03:00.973
with common complex disorders
like type 2 diabetes, for

00:03:00.974 --> 00:03:02.370
instance.

00:03:02.371 --> 00:03:04.600
But as we start to build
up these big bio banks, and

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.650
we start to sequence, that is
do DNA sequencing, on more

00:03:07.651 --> 00:03:10.280
of the people in those bio
banks, it becomes feasible to

00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:12.460
start thinking about moving in
the opposite direction as well.

00:03:12.461 --> 00:03:14.140
That is to find people and

00:03:14.141 --> 00:03:16.430
group them on the basis
of their genotype.

00:03:16.431 --> 00:03:19.460
So find, for instance, all
the people who have particular

00:03:19.461 --> 00:03:22.150
disruptive mutations in
a particular gene, group them on

00:03:22.151 --> 00:03:24.660
that basis and then look to see
what they have in common in

00:03:24.661 --> 00:03:27.570
terms of their traits, is what
we would call reverse genetics.

00:03:27.571 --> 00:03:30.250
It's a very common approach to
doing things in model organisms

00:03:30.251 --> 00:03:32.880
where we can actually
genetically engineer particular

00:03:32.881 --> 00:03:34.750
mice to lack a specific gene,
for instance, and

00:03:34.751 --> 00:03:36.460
then see what happens to them
when we knock that gene out.

00:03:36.461 --> 00:03:39.458
Now, of course, in humans,
we can't do genetic engineering.

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:41.689
But what we can do is take
advantage of the enormous amount

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:45.070
of genetic variation that exists
in the general population.

00:03:45.071 --> 00:03:48.552
And the amount of variation that
exists is genuinely staggering.

00:03:48.553 --> 00:03:51.126
You can calculate actually
relatively easily based on what

00:03:51.127 --> 00:03:52.567
we know about
the distribution of

00:03:52.568 --> 00:03:55.203
mutation rates across the genome
and how many people exist.

00:03:55.204 --> 00:03:59.202
That effectively, any given
single-nucleotide substitution,

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:02.006
that is at a particular
position in the genome or

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:05.581
changing to an A, or a C, or
a G, almost certainly exists out

00:04:05.582 --> 00:04:09.900
there among the 7 billion people
that exist in the population.

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:12.810
So that means humanity in effect
represents an enormous natural

00:04:12.811 --> 00:04:14.570
experiment where we have tried,

00:04:14.571 --> 00:04:18.215
certainly not the complete space
of human genomic sequence, but

00:04:18.216 --> 00:04:20.710
perturbations across many
different regions of the genome.

00:04:20.711 --> 00:04:23.570
And all we need to do to
understand exactly what effect

00:04:23.571 --> 00:04:26.410
each of those perturbations
have is sequence the genomes

00:04:26.411 --> 00:04:28.230
of all 7 billion people on
the face of the planet.

00:04:28.231 --> 00:04:30.040
Hook those into a big
electronic medical record

00:04:30.041 --> 00:04:31.330
system that allows
us to go back and

00:04:31.331 --> 00:04:33.580
recontact people based
on that information.

00:04:33.581 --> 00:04:35.380
And then systematically collect,
for instance,

00:04:35.381 --> 00:04:36.670
all the knockouts in
a particular gene and

00:04:36.671 --> 00:04:38.560
see what they have in common.

00:04:38.561 --> 00:04:40.107
Now, we are not,
as you guys know,

00:04:40.108 --> 00:04:42.946
anywhere close to being able to
sequence 7 billion genomes yet,

00:04:42.947 --> 00:04:44.717
although that will
happen eventually.

00:04:44.718 --> 00:04:47.598
But what we can do is to start
to accumulate information

00:04:47.599 --> 00:04:50.854
that has been collected across
now well over 2 million people

00:04:50.855 --> 00:04:53.548
who've had either their
whole genome sequenced or

00:04:53.549 --> 00:04:55.070
in many cases their exomes.

00:04:55.071 --> 00:04:57.260
That is a particular technology
that allows us to look

00:04:57.261 --> 00:04:59.648
just at the protein coding
bits of the genome.

00:04:59.649 --> 00:05:02.054
So this 2 million
number is a lower,

00:05:02.055 --> 00:05:03.925
sort of ball park estimate.

00:05:03.926 --> 00:05:06.765
These 2 million people have
been sequenced through a whole

00:05:06.766 --> 00:05:09.663
host of different academic and
commercial ventures and this

00:05:09.664 --> 00:05:12.503
information is being generated
in sites around the world,

00:05:12.504 --> 00:05:15.900
collected for a whole variety
of different projects.

00:05:15.901 --> 00:05:18.520
But just to give you an idea
about how fast that information

00:05:18.521 --> 00:05:21.040
is growing, curves like this I'm
sure have been presented earlier

00:05:21.041 --> 00:05:23.310
today, but this is information
just from one place

00:05:23.311 --> 00:05:25.900
the Broad Institute, again, a
couple of blocks down the road.

00:05:25.901 --> 00:05:29.181
You can see the growth in the
number of genomes that have been

00:05:29.182 --> 00:05:31.355
sequenced from 2009 to 2016.

00:05:31.356 --> 00:05:33.585
This information's about,
I guess about 8 months,

00:05:33.586 --> 00:05:35.050
10 months out of date.

00:05:35.051 --> 00:05:37.360
Over here we have the number
of exomes sequenced.

00:05:37.361 --> 00:05:39.880
And the two bits of information
you should be taking from these

00:05:39.881 --> 00:05:42.302
curves is firstly the number at
the top, which it tells us we

00:05:42.303 --> 00:05:44.919
had at the end of last year
sequenced about 70,000 genomes

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.782
and over a quarter of
a million human exomes.

00:05:46.783 --> 00:05:48.442
It's a staggering
amount of data, but

00:05:48.443 --> 00:05:49.710
also the shape of this curve.

00:05:49.711 --> 00:05:52.264
In 2016 we sequenced more
genomes than in all years

00:05:52.265 --> 00:05:54.190
previously combined.

00:05:54.191 --> 00:05:56.770
And so the trajectory at which
we are generating this data is

00:05:56.771 --> 00:05:57.480
truly staggering.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.590
Now, ideally what we wanna do
is to take all these 2 million

00:06:01.591 --> 00:06:02.710
sequenced individuals and

00:06:02.711 --> 00:06:05.690
house them all together in one
big unified bio bank that would

00:06:05.691 --> 00:06:08.470
allows us to link between
genotype and phenotype, but

00:06:08.471 --> 00:06:10.830
this turns out to be difficult
for all sorts of reasons.

00:06:10.831 --> 00:06:14.431
There's mundane obstacles
associated with moving data from

00:06:14.432 --> 00:06:15.751
one place to another.

00:06:15.752 --> 00:06:18.654
It turns out because these
samples have been collected over

00:06:18.655 --> 00:06:21.964
many decades, often well before
they were sequenced, the consent

00:06:21.965 --> 00:06:24.983
that they were consented under
is often inadequate to justify

00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:27.030
very large scale data sharing.

00:06:27.031 --> 00:06:30.558
And in fact, a lot of my life is
dealing with inadequate consent

00:06:30.559 --> 00:06:32.100
and data use permissions.

00:06:32.101 --> 00:06:35.161
There are objections to data
sharing, so much of this data

00:06:35.162 --> 00:06:37.534
has been generated in
an industry setting and

00:06:37.535 --> 00:06:39.730
that is often regarded
as a trade secret.

00:06:39.731 --> 00:06:41.377
You guys, I'm sure,
will be shocked to learn

00:06:41.378 --> 00:06:43.366
there are even some academics
that are not fully on board

00:06:43.367 --> 00:06:46.000
with the idea of sharing data,
so this can be challenging.

00:06:46.001 --> 00:06:48.130
And then a final problem that we
face is the fact that different

00:06:48.131 --> 00:06:51.370
centers who generate this
data tend to process and

00:06:51.371 --> 00:06:54.830
perform variant polling across
that data in idiosyncratic ways.

00:06:54.831 --> 00:06:57.450
So if we were to just take the
outputs of all of each of these

00:06:57.451 --> 00:06:59.960
different centers and try to
merge together this matrix of 2

00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:01.850
million sets of genotypes,

00:07:01.851 --> 00:07:05.000
we would end up with a variant
core set that was dominated

00:07:05.001 --> 00:07:07.550
by technical differences
between centers, as opposed to

00:07:07.551 --> 00:07:09.360
interesting biological
differences between people,

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:10.210
which of course is not useful.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.377
So to resolve at least some of
these problems we have been

00:07:14.378 --> 00:07:17.524
working I guess for about the
last four years on building up

00:07:17.525 --> 00:07:20.159
large reference databases
of human variation,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.652
leveraging the expertise and the
data that exists at the Broad.

00:07:23.653 --> 00:07:26.331
So when we started this exercise
back in 2012 there were two

00:07:26.332 --> 00:07:28.320
large public data sets
of human variation.

00:07:28.321 --> 00:07:30.160
We had the 1,000
Genomes Project.

00:07:30.161 --> 00:07:31.479
Which contained about two and

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.010
half thousand, low coverage,
whole human genomes from

00:07:34.011 --> 00:07:35.706
a variety of different
populations.

00:07:35.707 --> 00:07:37.588
It's a fantastic project.

00:07:37.589 --> 00:07:40.627
We also have the NHLBI's Exome
Sequencing Project, which has

00:07:40.628 --> 00:07:43.553
generated exome sequencing from
about six and half thousand

00:07:43.554 --> 00:07:46.724
individuals from either European
or African American descent.

00:07:46.725 --> 00:07:49.871
And so to cut a very long story
short, we began analyzing rare

00:07:49.872 --> 00:07:53.144
disease patients using these
resources and rapidly found they

00:07:53.145 --> 00:07:55.858
were too small, and in many
cases, too old to be good

00:07:55.859 --> 00:07:59.360
comparison data sets for
our rare disease patients.

00:07:59.361 --> 00:08:03.130
So we built, over a relatively
painful 18 month period,

00:08:03.131 --> 00:08:05.830
our first large core
set of human exomes.

00:08:05.831 --> 00:08:08.845
This is called the Exome
Aggregation Consortium, or

00:08:08.846 --> 00:08:11.800
ExAC data set,
that was released back in 2014.

00:08:11.801 --> 00:08:14.522
And that contained information
from just over 60,000

00:08:14.523 --> 00:08:15.222
human exomes.

00:08:15.223 --> 00:08:18.275
And then almost to the day two
years later, in October last

00:08:18.276 --> 00:08:20.766
year, we released
an updated version of ExAC,

00:08:20.767 --> 00:08:24.214
this is now called the Genome
Aggregation database, or gnomAD.

00:08:24.215 --> 00:08:25.908
Because we like
slightly curious and

00:08:25.909 --> 00:08:27.826
idiosyncratically capitalized
names.

00:08:27.827 --> 00:08:30.543
And this now contains about
twice the number of human exomes

00:08:30.544 --> 00:08:32.009
and for the first time in Nomad,

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.740
we've also included a whole
collection of human genomes.

00:08:34.741 --> 00:08:37.236
So providing information about
the distribution of variation

00:08:37.237 --> 00:08:38.178
across the noncoding,

00:08:38.179 --> 00:08:40.260
that is the non-protein
coding bits of the genome.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.490
So it's important to note
that we do not sequence these

00:08:44.491 --> 00:08:45.060
samples.

00:08:45.061 --> 00:08:48.330
These data all come from
various studies, and I'll list

00:08:48.331 --> 00:08:51.340
the PIs and where these
samples come from in a minute.

00:08:51.341 --> 00:08:54.330
For many different studies,
we aggregate that data and

00:08:54.331 --> 00:08:56.700
we put it through the same
processing pipeline.

00:08:56.701 --> 00:08:58.093
Then we do what we call,

00:08:58.094 --> 00:09:02.010
joint variant coding across all
samples simultaneously or less.

00:09:02.011 --> 00:09:05.080
And that allows us to get
a harmonized code set across

00:09:05.081 --> 00:09:06.550
these large numbers of samples.

00:09:06.551 --> 00:09:09.030
And this is a testament
to the engineering and

00:09:09.031 --> 00:09:11.050
scaling work that's been
done at the Broad Institute.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.728
So this is brief summary
of the pipeline.

00:09:14.729 --> 00:09:16.848
I'm not gonna go through
the details about how a variant

00:09:16.849 --> 00:09:17.850
calling pipeline works.

00:09:17.851 --> 00:09:20.430
I don't think it's hugely
relevant for this audience.

00:09:20.431 --> 00:09:22.070
I guess it is worth
emphasizing on this slide,

00:09:22.071 --> 00:09:24.170
we start with raw data.

00:09:24.171 --> 00:09:27.610
We go through an individual
sample variant,

00:09:27.611 --> 00:09:30.050
genotyping variant
calling approach.

00:09:30.051 --> 00:09:32.690
We then do joint genotyping
across large numbers of samples.

00:09:32.691 --> 00:09:34.170
There's a variant
recalibration approach.

00:09:34.171 --> 00:09:36.035
And then we have
this final call set,

00:09:36.036 --> 00:09:38.381
this very big variant call file,
or VCF file,

00:09:38.382 --> 00:09:40.800
that stores all the data
across these samples.

00:09:40.801 --> 00:09:42.194
So the thing I did
wanna mention, though,

00:09:42.195 --> 00:09:42.980
is this is a lot of data.

00:09:42.981 --> 00:09:45.788
So the raw data that's gone into
this project over the exome data

00:09:45.789 --> 00:09:48.336
which is on the left-hand side,
and the genome data on the

00:09:48.337 --> 00:09:51.210
right-hand side is about three
petabytes of raw sequence data.

00:09:51.211 --> 00:09:54.205
And that condenses down to about
a 40 terabyte variant core file

00:09:54.206 --> 00:09:56.819
which is actually still a
staggeringly large file that we

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:58.410
have to work with.

00:09:58.411 --> 00:10:00.250
Many people were involved
in generating and

00:10:00.251 --> 00:10:01.250
analyzing these data.

00:10:01.251 --> 00:10:02.933
And I wanted to
particularly call out

00:10:02.934 --> 00:10:05.700
the Broad Data Sciences Platform
for building the tools that

00:10:05.701 --> 00:10:07.690
allowed us to call
variants in this scale.

00:10:07.691 --> 00:10:10.980
But also incredibly
importantly a tool kit called

00:10:10.981 --> 00:10:13.775
the Hale System which was
built at the Broad Institute.

00:10:13.776 --> 00:10:17.330
Which Alex here in the room is a
representative of the Hale team.

00:10:17.331 --> 00:10:20.000
And Hale was absolutely critical
in being able to go from this 40

00:10:20.001 --> 00:10:20.530
terabyte VCF, and

00:10:20.531 --> 00:10:23.290
actually do important things
with it like quality control,

00:10:23.291 --> 00:10:24.760
and assess the variants, and

00:10:24.761 --> 00:10:27.060
release data in a useful
way to the public.

00:10:27.061 --> 00:10:27.980
And a huge thanks

00:10:27.981 --> 00:10:29.850
to all the members of the team
who contributed to this as well.

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.450
So it's worth emphasizing
who's actually ended up in

00:10:33.451 --> 00:10:34.090
these databases.

00:10:34.091 --> 00:10:37.220
So we have these rough, over
135,000 people across the exomes

00:10:37.221 --> 00:10:40.450
and the genomes who were
in the gnomAD database.

00:10:40.451 --> 00:10:42.230
These people are not
necessarily well.

00:10:42.231 --> 00:10:45.510
They come primarily from
case control studies of

00:10:45.511 --> 00:10:48.430
relatively common complex
adult onset diseases.

00:10:48.431 --> 00:10:51.310
The big hitters in terms of
the major diseases included in

00:10:51.311 --> 00:10:53.380
the database
are type 2 diabetes,

00:10:53.381 --> 00:10:56.530
early onset myocardial
infarction or heart attack,

00:10:56.531 --> 00:10:59.750
and neuropsychiatric conditions
like schizophrenia and bipolar.

00:10:59.751 --> 00:11:01.900
So about half the people are
controls for those studies and

00:11:01.901 --> 00:11:03.550
about half the people are cases.

00:11:03.551 --> 00:11:06.180
Now we have as best we can
depleted this resource

00:11:06.181 --> 00:11:08.740
of people known to have
severe pediatric disease.

00:11:08.741 --> 00:11:11.194
So the types of underlying
diseases that Gil talked about

00:11:11.195 --> 00:11:13.380
as well as their first
degree relatives.

00:11:13.381 --> 00:11:16.190
But of course some individuals
in this cohort will still

00:11:16.191 --> 00:11:18.730
be ill or will go on
to become ill later on

00:11:18.731 --> 00:11:20.680
through adult onset
rare disorders.

00:11:20.681 --> 00:11:22.900
However, we think that the
levels of severe disease within

00:11:22.901 --> 00:11:24.950
this population are probably
broadly comparable to

00:11:24.951 --> 00:11:26.480
the general
population as a whole.

00:11:26.481 --> 00:11:28.690
So this is a reasonable
reference data set if you're

00:11:28.691 --> 00:11:32.020
interested in figuring out, if a
variant that is present in your

00:11:32.021 --> 00:11:35.040
rare disease patient is present
in the general population.

00:11:35.041 --> 00:11:37.820
And if so, what frequency it
has within that population.

00:11:37.821 --> 00:11:39.769
And I won't go into detail
about some of the work

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:40.610
we're doing here.

00:11:40.611 --> 00:11:43.232
But about 40% of the samples
have some clinical data

00:11:43.233 --> 00:11:43.860
available.

00:11:43.861 --> 00:11:46.462
In fact, about a third of
the samples have consent that is

00:11:46.463 --> 00:11:48.260
consistent with recontact.

00:11:48.261 --> 00:11:49.970
So for
a subset of these individuals,

00:11:49.971 --> 00:11:50.950
we can actually go back.

00:11:50.951 --> 00:11:52.270
If we find interesting
genotypes,

00:11:52.271 --> 00:11:53.520
we can actually go back and

00:11:53.521 --> 00:11:55.310
see what sorts of phenotypes
they have, or at least

00:11:55.311 --> 00:11:57.379
get some limited information
about their clinical status.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.082
So I'm showing this slide purely
to indicate that this has

00:12:02.083 --> 00:12:04.598
been a project that is
involved in an enormous number

00:12:04.599 --> 00:12:07.286
of data donators, so
principal investigators who have

00:12:07.287 --> 00:12:09.530
allowed their data to be
used in this process.

00:12:09.531 --> 00:12:12.510
I think at last count we're up
to about 107 PIs who signed

00:12:12.511 --> 00:12:16.150
an MOU that allows their data
to be used in this project.

00:12:16.151 --> 00:12:17.630
So a huge thanks to them and,
of course,

00:12:17.631 --> 00:12:20.050
to our funders for
making this possible.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:23.060
So this is

00:12:23.061 --> 00:12:25.940
one of the first things we did
across the gnomAD data set.

00:12:25.941 --> 00:12:27.770
It's a principal
component analysis,

00:12:27.771 --> 00:12:30.770
basically just showing the first
three principal components,

00:12:30.771 --> 00:12:32.250
which correspond,
broadly speaking,

00:12:32.251 --> 00:12:35.230
to geographical ancestry
across the gnomAD individuals.

00:12:35.231 --> 00:12:38.246
Here we've generated this based
on about just over 50,000

00:12:38.247 --> 00:12:40.220
well-called single
nucleotide variants.

00:12:40.221 --> 00:12:43.198
And we have inferred the
population labels using a random

00:12:43.199 --> 00:12:46.859
forest approach with a set of
about 40,000 people of so-called

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:47.800
known ancestry.

00:12:47.801 --> 00:12:50.558
So what we can see in this plot
is that we have a pretty diverse

00:12:50.559 --> 00:12:53.330
set of individuals who
have been sequenced here.

00:12:53.331 --> 00:12:55.820
A big cloud of individuals
of European ancestry,

00:12:55.821 --> 00:12:58.450
we have clusters corresponding
to Finnish ancestry and

00:12:58.451 --> 00:12:59.720
Ashkenazi Jewish.

00:12:59.721 --> 00:13:02.495
Big cloud up here of South Asian
individuals, and we also have

00:13:02.496 --> 00:13:05.350
clusters corresponding to
individuals of Latino ancestry,

00:13:05.351 --> 00:13:10.980
African, and African-American
as well as East Asian ancestry.

00:13:10.981 --> 00:13:12.960
So this is by no means a
comprehensive sampling of human

00:13:12.961 --> 00:13:13.660
genetic diversity.

00:13:13.661 --> 00:13:14.670
In fact we're not even close.

00:13:14.671 --> 00:13:15.650
We're missing, for instance,

00:13:15.651 --> 00:13:17.860
enormous swathes of
diversity in Africa.

00:13:17.861 --> 00:13:19.148
The Middle East is missing,

00:13:19.149 --> 00:13:21.671
and this is because of the
ascertainment vices that have

00:13:21.672 --> 00:13:24.571
gone into generating the samples
that are going to have gone into

00:13:24.572 --> 00:13:25.280
this dataset.

00:13:25.281 --> 00:13:28.228
But what we do have is
thousands of individuals, for

00:13:28.229 --> 00:13:31.320
each of a set of relatively
major continental groups.

00:13:31.321 --> 00:13:32.520
And this is really
important if we wanna

00:13:32.521 --> 00:13:35.720
be able to assign relatively
confidently population frequency

00:13:35.721 --> 00:13:37.190
estimates for
variants that we find in

00:13:37.191 --> 00:13:38.660
rare disease patients
from those continents.

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:43.186
And if you're interested
in accessing the data,

00:13:43.187 --> 00:13:45.548
one of the key principles of
ExAc and gnomAD, actually right

00:13:45.549 --> 00:13:48.047
from the very beginning was that
we wanted this to be a resource,

00:13:48.048 --> 00:13:48.972
not just for us, but for

00:13:48.973 --> 00:13:51.230
anyone who wanted to look up
variants on their patients.

00:13:51.231 --> 00:13:53.810
So you can go to
gnomad.broadinstitute.org.

00:13:53.811 --> 00:13:55.040
You can look up
your favorite gene.

00:13:55.041 --> 00:13:56.880
I’m sure you guys all
have a favorite gene.

00:13:56.881 --> 00:13:59.900
When you do, you will find these
blue peaks that correspond to

00:13:59.901 --> 00:14:01.940
the coverage across the exomes.

00:14:01.941 --> 00:14:04.410
And the green line represents
the coverage across the genomes,

00:14:04.411 --> 00:14:06.760
across the exomes of
that particular gene.

00:14:06.761 --> 00:14:09.390
Below that is a big long
table of variants that tells

00:14:09.391 --> 00:14:11.370
you every variant that we have
discovered within that gene

00:14:11.371 --> 00:14:13.630
across both the coding and
the noncoding regions.

00:14:13.631 --> 00:14:14.980
And you can click on those
to learn more about how

00:14:14.981 --> 00:14:16.550
common they are in
the population and

00:14:16.551 --> 00:14:18.240
how confident we are that
they're actually real.

00:14:18.241 --> 00:14:21.260
Including for most variants
actually a snapshot of the role

00:14:21.261 --> 00:14:22.490
read support,

00:14:22.491 --> 00:14:25.650
telling us that that variant
is actually a genuine variant.

00:14:25.651 --> 00:14:28.060
I mean, you can also download
a site's PCF that gives you

00:14:28.061 --> 00:14:30.190
a complete list of every
variant that we've called

00:14:30.191 --> 00:14:31.860
as well as its frequency
across populations.

00:14:31.861 --> 00:14:36.520
And that's available for use in
any setting without embargo or

00:14:36.521 --> 00:14:37.610
any restrictions
whatsoever on use.

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:42.600
So I wanted to talk about some
of scientific things that we

00:14:42.601 --> 00:14:46.160
can do once we have a collection
of 135,000 exomes and genomes.

00:14:46.161 --> 00:14:46.981
One of the things that

00:14:46.982 --> 00:14:49.213
we spent a lot of time thinking
about is ways that we can look

00:14:49.214 --> 00:14:51.830
at not just the variants that
are present in this data set.

00:14:51.831 --> 00:14:53.990
But also the variants that
are missing from the data set.

00:14:53.991 --> 00:14:55.480
In that sense,

00:14:55.481 --> 00:14:57.539
I mean, informatively
missing from the data set.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.250
And so to do this,
we worked very closely with

00:15:00.251 --> 00:15:03.350
Caitlin Symoka, a graduate
student in Mark Daily's lab.

00:15:03.351 --> 00:15:05.966
She had developed a statistical
model that allowed her to

00:15:05.967 --> 00:15:08.258
predict, actually with
quite high confidence,

00:15:08.259 --> 00:15:11.041
given non-mutation rates how
many variants of a particular

00:15:11.042 --> 00:15:13.549
functional class we should
expect to see in each gene in

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:16.057
the genome in a collection
of 60,000 people, or

00:15:16.058 --> 00:15:17.280
135,000 people.

00:15:17.281 --> 00:15:19.792
And so this allows us to say,
you can see in this plot here

00:15:19.793 --> 00:15:22.037
for a variant type like
synonymous variants, for

00:15:22.038 --> 00:15:24.710
instance, which are variants
that are found within coding

00:15:24.711 --> 00:15:27.543
regions, but don't change the
amino acid sequence, how many

00:15:27.544 --> 00:15:30.120
synonymous variants we would
expect to see in each gene.

00:15:30.121 --> 00:15:32.670
That's shown here on the x-axis
under Caitlin's model.

00:15:32.671 --> 00:15:34.010
Each dot here is a gene.

00:15:34.011 --> 00:15:36.982
And on the y-axis, we have the
number of variants of that type

00:15:36.983 --> 00:15:40.140
that were actually observed
in the 60,000 people in ExAC.

00:15:40.141 --> 00:15:41.457
And you can see that the fit for

00:15:41.458 --> 00:15:43.409
synonymous experience
is extremely good,

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:44.870
an r squared of about 0.98.

00:15:44.871 --> 00:15:45.480
And so

00:15:45.481 --> 00:15:48.100
that tells us that this model
is actually very well calibrated

00:15:48.101 --> 00:15:51.250
for variants that aren't subject
to strong natural selection.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.030
But that relationship
begins to break down.

00:15:55.031 --> 00:15:56.940
In fact, it breaks down very
severely as we start looking at

00:15:56.941 --> 00:15:59.300
variants that do actually have
a functional impact on genes.

00:15:59.301 --> 00:16:01.030
So here we're focusing
on genes that are,

00:16:01.031 --> 00:16:02.270
what we call,
loss of function genes.

00:16:02.271 --> 00:16:03.470
That is they're
predicted to break

00:16:03.471 --> 00:16:05.540
the function of
a protein coding gene.

00:16:05.541 --> 00:16:06.650
And you can see

00:16:06.651 --> 00:16:09.000
here that the relationship
here is far from linear.

00:16:09.001 --> 00:16:11.021
Almost all the genes in
the genome have fewer loss of

00:16:11.022 --> 00:16:13.280
function variants that we
would expect to see by chance.

00:16:13.281 --> 00:16:14.490
And it is not surprising at all.

00:16:14.491 --> 00:16:16.696
What it tells us is that for
most genes in the genome,

00:16:16.697 --> 00:16:18.210
loss of function
variants are bad.

00:16:18.211 --> 00:16:18.898
And so as a result,

00:16:18.899 --> 00:16:21.990
they tend to be removed from the
population by natural selection.

00:16:21.991 --> 00:16:24.160
What's cool about this, though,
is that with 60,000 people and

00:16:24.161 --> 00:16:25.290
even more so
with 135,000 people,

00:16:25.291 --> 00:16:29.380
we can actually say for many
of the genes in the genome how

00:16:29.381 --> 00:16:31.750
far below the line
does that gene fall.

00:16:31.751 --> 00:16:33.800
And therefore, how strong
is the action of natural

00:16:33.801 --> 00:16:35.860
selection that's acting on
what's the function variants

00:16:35.861 --> 00:16:37.170
in that particular gene.

00:16:37.171 --> 00:16:39.764
So that really gives us a
measure about how critical that

00:16:39.765 --> 00:16:41.707
gene is and
how bad it is when you break it.

00:16:41.708 --> 00:16:43.852
So just to give you two
examples, this is a gene called

00:16:43.853 --> 00:16:46.427
DYNC1H1 where we know exactly
what happens when you break it,

00:16:46.428 --> 00:16:48.861
heterozygous Missense and loss
of function variance in this

00:16:48.862 --> 00:16:51.438
gene result in some pretty nasty
neurodevelopmental disorders

00:16:51.439 --> 00:16:54.520
including seizure disorders and
intellectual disability.

00:16:54.521 --> 00:16:56.460
Kaitlin's model is very
well calibrated for

00:16:56.461 --> 00:16:58.030
synonymous variance
in this gene.

00:16:58.031 --> 00:17:00.085
The gene is missing about
two-thirds of the missense

00:17:00.086 --> 00:17:02.680
variants and virtually all of
the loss of function variance

00:17:02.681 --> 00:17:04.530
that we would expect
to be see by chance.

00:17:04.531 --> 00:17:06.349
We've spent,
would expect to see 161.

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:08.020
In fact, we only see four.

00:17:08.021 --> 00:17:09.810
And of those four,
two of them are artifacts.

00:17:09.811 --> 00:17:10.800
One is a sequencing error.

00:17:10.801 --> 00:17:12.150
One is an annotation error.

00:17:12.151 --> 00:17:14.000
There are in fact two people
who appear to be carrying

00:17:14.001 --> 00:17:16.070
loss of function
variants in this gene.

00:17:16.071 --> 00:17:17.610
We don't have finished updated
data about them and in fact,

00:17:17.611 --> 00:17:20.150
they may actually suffer
from these disorders.

00:17:20.151 --> 00:17:22.280
So that's when we know what
happens when you break the gene.

00:17:22.281 --> 00:17:24.650
This is a gene where we don't
know what happens in humans.

00:17:24.651 --> 00:17:27.230
And this gene, UBL5, we know is
involved in ubiquinone pathway.

00:17:27.231 --> 00:17:28.370
We know if you knock
it out in mice,

00:17:28.371 --> 00:17:30.250
it kills them during
embryogenesis.

00:17:30.251 --> 00:17:32.427
But there's no known human
loss of function phenotype.

00:17:32.428 --> 00:17:35.339
But it is has a profile that's
extremely similar to [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.139
on H1, telling us actually
with very high confidence that

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:40.771
heterozygous loss of function
mutations in this gene do

00:17:40.772 --> 00:17:43.790
something, probably
something pretty nasty.

00:17:43.791 --> 00:17:47.003
Probably resulting in some kind
of relatively severe human

00:17:47.004 --> 00:17:48.167
disease phenotype.

00:17:48.168 --> 00:17:50.049
The fact that we don't know what
that disease phenotype is yet

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:50.964
could mean one of two things.

00:17:50.965 --> 00:17:53.623
We just haven't sequenced
the right people yet or

00:17:53.624 --> 00:17:56.760
it could be that, in fact,
this results in embryo loss.

00:17:56.761 --> 00:17:59.200
So heterozygous loss of
function results in people

00:17:59.201 --> 00:18:00.520
dying before they can
actually be born.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:03.340
So overall we identified
more than 3,000

00:18:03.341 --> 00:18:04.950
genes with a near complete loss

00:18:04.951 --> 00:18:07.400
of loss of function variants
compared to expectations.

00:18:07.401 --> 00:18:10.268
We call these high in genes
with a high probability of loss

00:18:10.269 --> 00:18:12.193
of function intolerance or
high pLI.

00:18:12.194 --> 00:18:13.946
And that includes almost
all of the known genes,

00:18:13.947 --> 00:18:16.400
where knocking at a single copy
is enough to cause disease.

00:18:16.401 --> 00:18:18.290
But importantly,
more than 70% of these genes,

00:18:18.291 --> 00:18:19.460
we have no idea what they do.

00:18:19.461 --> 00:18:21.315
There's no associated
human phenotype.

00:18:21.316 --> 00:18:22.250
In fact, for most of them,

00:18:22.251 --> 00:18:24.300
we don't know what
the molecular function is.

00:18:24.301 --> 00:18:26.981
And so we now prioritize
the genes extraordinarily

00:18:26.982 --> 00:18:29.859
heavily when we start looking
at disease phenotypes.

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:32.603
And this has already proven
extremely useful in narrowing

00:18:32.604 --> 00:18:34.787
down on genes in autism,
schizophrenia, and

00:18:34.788 --> 00:18:37.367
a whole range of other very
nasty pediatric diseases.

00:18:39.102 --> 00:18:42.104
So the last thing I wanted to
talk about was an example of how

00:18:42.105 --> 00:18:45.354
we can use these large cohorts
to understand the precise impact

00:18:45.355 --> 00:18:48.193
of individual variance within
a gene on disease risk.

00:18:48.194 --> 00:18:51.739
And this work that's been led
by Eric Minikel, this was a very

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:55.900
personal project, for reasons
I'll describe in a minute.

00:18:55.901 --> 00:18:56.580
And what

00:18:56.581 --> 00:18:59.590
happened while Eric Minikel
was an analyst in my lab.

00:18:59.591 --> 00:19:02.360
So Eric came to my lab extremely
motivated to work on a gene call

00:19:02.361 --> 00:19:06.180
PRNP which includes
the prion protein.

00:19:06.181 --> 00:19:08.419
And his motivation for
this was his wife, Sonia,

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:09.333
who's shown here.

00:19:09.334 --> 00:19:10.673
Sonia is shown with her mother,

00:19:10.674 --> 00:19:13.306
this photo was taken at the
beginning of 2010 when Sonia's

00:19:13.307 --> 00:19:14.706
mother was completely healthy.

00:19:14.707 --> 00:19:16.131
Over the course of that year,

00:19:16.132 --> 00:19:18.821
she underwent a pretty
terrifying cognitive decline.

00:19:18.822 --> 00:19:20.316
Went into frank dementia, and

00:19:20.317 --> 00:19:22.450
awfully passed away at
the end of that year.

00:19:22.451 --> 00:19:24.961
So it was about a nine-month
period between the onset of

00:19:24.962 --> 00:19:26.090
symptoms and her dying.

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:28.103
At the time that she died,

00:19:28.104 --> 00:19:29.995
it was completely unclear
what had actually killed her.

00:19:29.996 --> 00:19:32.566
But on an autopsy, it was found
that her brain was full of

00:19:32.567 --> 00:19:36.100
accumulations of a particular
protein called prion protein.

00:19:36.101 --> 00:19:38.360
This is, in some cases,
a genetic disease.

00:19:38.361 --> 00:19:41.430
So her prion protein
gene was sequenced.

00:19:41.431 --> 00:19:44.450
She was found to carry a
mutation that was known to cause

00:19:44.451 --> 00:19:47.144
in a dominant way, a very
severe disease prion disease

00:19:47.145 --> 00:19:48.840
called Fatal Familial Insomnia.

00:19:48.841 --> 00:19:50.789
So Sonia had a 50% chance
of actually carrying

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:52.200
that same mutation.

00:19:52.201 --> 00:19:53.844
She then had, was faced
with a choice of whether or

00:19:53.845 --> 00:19:55.070
not she wanted to
be tested herself.

00:19:55.071 --> 00:19:56.640
Her mother had died in her 50s.

00:19:56.641 --> 00:19:58.800
She was at the time
in her early 30s.

00:19:58.801 --> 00:20:00.860
She and Eric thought about it
and then decided to go ahead and

00:20:00.861 --> 00:20:01.510
get tested.

00:20:01.511 --> 00:20:04.210
And in early 2011,
Sonia learned that she too

00:20:04.211 --> 00:20:06.300
carries the same variant
that killed her mother.

00:20:06.301 --> 00:20:07.240
So as far as we know,

00:20:07.241 --> 00:20:09.430
everyone who carries this
particular variant has a 100%

00:20:09.431 --> 00:20:11.050
chance of going on to
die from prion disease.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.910
There's many ways of responding
to this kind of incredibly

00:20:15.911 --> 00:20:17.638
catastrophic information.

00:20:17.639 --> 00:20:20.030
Eric and Sonia responded in
a way that I, still to this day,

00:20:20.031 --> 00:20:20.790
find inspiring.

00:20:20.791 --> 00:20:22.000
They quit their jobs.

00:20:22.001 --> 00:20:23.770
Eric at the time
was a town planner.

00:20:23.771 --> 00:20:26.390
Sonia was a lawyer,
a Harvard-trained lawyer.

00:20:26.391 --> 00:20:28.130
So they abandoned their
respective careers and

00:20:28.131 --> 00:20:30.618
retrained completely as
biomedical scientists.

00:20:30.619 --> 00:20:32.390
Initially with Sonia
training in the wet lab,

00:20:32.391 --> 00:20:35.110
and Eric focusing on
bioinformatics work and that's

00:20:35.111 --> 00:20:37.150
what they continue doing to this
day, as I'll show you later.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:41.280
So when Eric came to my lab,
what he wanted to know more than

00:20:41.281 --> 00:20:43.368
anything was an answer
to this question.

00:20:43.369 --> 00:20:45.050
Given that Sonia carries
this particular mutation,

00:20:45.051 --> 00:20:48.400
how likely is it that she will
go on to get prion disease.

00:20:48.401 --> 00:20:49.848
This turns out to be
a little bit uncertain,

00:20:49.849 --> 00:20:51.306
it is a question of what
we call penetrance.

00:20:51.307 --> 00:20:54.102
That is the probability that a
carrier of a particular disease,

00:20:54.103 --> 00:20:57.240
genotype, will actually go
on to manifest that disease.

00:20:57.241 --> 00:20:59.060
This is known to be
incomplete and variable for

00:20:59.061 --> 00:21:00.780
many disease causing mutations.

00:21:00.781 --> 00:21:03.890
So, in BRCA1 for instance,
we know that woman who have

00:21:03.891 --> 00:21:06.750
variance in this gene will go on
to have somewhere between a 60

00:21:06.751 --> 00:21:09.000
to 80% chance of
contracting breast cancer.

00:21:09.001 --> 00:21:11.090
But that has a big error
bars associated with it,

00:21:11.091 --> 00:21:13.740
and not every woman who has a
BRCA1 mutation will go on to die

00:21:13.741 --> 00:21:14.960
from breast cancer.

00:21:14.961 --> 00:21:16.940
And in fact, for
most disease-causing variants,

00:21:16.941 --> 00:21:18.900
even ones that are relatively
well-established,

00:21:18.901 --> 00:21:21.000
we don't actually have a good
quantitative estimate of what

00:21:21.001 --> 00:21:21.640
that penetrance is.

00:21:21.641 --> 00:21:24.510
And that's also true for
prion disease.

00:21:24.511 --> 00:21:26.671
So prion diseases we
know are relatively rare,

00:21:26.672 --> 00:21:28.461
lifetime risk about 1 in 5,000.

00:21:28.462 --> 00:21:30.634
85% of the cases, we have
no idea what causes them,

00:21:30.635 --> 00:21:32.160
they appear to be sporadic.

00:21:32.161 --> 00:21:34.560
But as I mentioned,
15% of these cases are genetic,

00:21:34.561 --> 00:21:36.921
a notorious 1% of prion
disease cases are acquired.

00:21:36.922 --> 00:21:39.893
And they can be acquired through
ingestion of contaminated meat

00:21:39.894 --> 00:21:42.865
for instance qs was the case for
the variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob

00:21:42.866 --> 00:21:45.740
disease outbreak in the UK, it
was caused by mad cow disease.

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:50.435
The genetic cases are due
to set of over sixty

00:21:50.436 --> 00:21:53.230
known dominant gain of function
mutations in the gene.

00:21:53.231 --> 00:21:55.155
And what's amazing about
this disease is that we have

00:21:55.156 --> 00:21:56.537
incredibly good
surveillance data.

00:21:56.538 --> 00:21:59.347
Basically everyone who is
diagnosed with prion disease

00:21:59.348 --> 00:22:01.618
gets reported to
a surveillance center, and

00:22:01.619 --> 00:22:04.740
for most of those individuals,
the prion gene is sequenced.

00:22:04.741 --> 00:22:07.360
And so we have an almost
complete collection of genetic

00:22:07.361 --> 00:22:10.870
information from diagnosed
prion disease cases across

00:22:10.871 --> 00:22:11.510
the western world.

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:15.570
And so what Eric said about
doing was collecting a set of

00:22:15.571 --> 00:22:17.950
information that would allow
us to compare the frequency of

00:22:17.951 --> 00:22:21.040
these mutations in prion
disease cases versus controls.

00:22:21.041 --> 00:22:23.270
So we started with a set
of population controls and

00:22:23.271 --> 00:22:26.450
here we reasoned that completely
penetrant versions in aggregate

00:22:26.451 --> 00:22:29.020
for a dominant disease should be
no more common in the population

00:22:29.021 --> 00:22:30.060
than the disease
that they cause.

00:22:30.061 --> 00:22:32.573
So that means that we can run
analysis even in a set of

00:22:32.574 --> 00:22:35.379
samples like ExAC, where we
don't know exactly whether

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:37.316
people will go on to
get prion disease.

00:22:37.317 --> 00:22:40.191
But we can assume relatively
confidently that the frequency

00:22:40.192 --> 00:22:42.220
of prion disease causing
mutations in that

00:22:42.221 --> 00:22:44.923
population will be no higher
than the actual frequency of

00:22:44.924 --> 00:22:46.080
prion disease itself.

00:22:46.081 --> 00:22:49.580
So we collected our 60,000
ExAC exomes all of whom had

00:22:49.581 --> 00:22:52.710
effectively perfect coverage for
the prion gene.

00:22:52.711 --> 00:22:55.270
We're also able to work with
23andMe, to bring in information

00:22:55.271 --> 00:22:57.290
from about half a million
of their customers,

00:22:57.291 --> 00:22:58.780
all of whom have
been genotyped for

00:22:58.781 --> 00:23:02.250
16 reportedly disease-causing
stamps within that gene.

00:23:02.251 --> 00:23:04.950
And then in a series of
feats of pretty remarkable

00:23:04.951 --> 00:23:08.760
political jiu-jitsu, Eric was
able to collect over 10,500

00:23:08.761 --> 00:23:12.840
cases of prion disease, who had
had the PR and P gene sequence.

00:23:12.841 --> 00:23:14.902
This constitutes pretty
much every known or

00:23:14.903 --> 00:23:18.590
probable prion disease case that
has appeared in the US, Europe,

00:23:18.591 --> 00:23:21.270
Australia, and Japan for
the last 15 to 20 years.

00:23:21.271 --> 00:23:23.560
It's the biggest collection of
rare disease cases that I have

00:23:23.561 --> 00:23:24.130
ever worked with.

00:23:24.131 --> 00:23:25.670
And probably the biggest
collection of cases

00:23:25.671 --> 00:23:27.583
that we'll ever get for
something like prion disease.

00:23:27.584 --> 00:23:29.750
So this is a really remarkable
data set to do a comparison on.

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:34.141
And so we can calculate pretty
easily how many individuals we

00:23:34.142 --> 00:23:37.270
would expect to see in our
60,000 ExAC individuals, who

00:23:37.271 --> 00:23:40.471
actually do have real variants
that will cause a prion disease.

00:23:40.472 --> 00:23:43.041
And if you multiply these
slightly hand wavy numbers out,

00:23:43.042 --> 00:23:45.505
you end up with a number that
is somewhere between one and

00:23:45.506 --> 00:23:48.075
two individuals who carry a
mutation that will go on to kill

00:23:48.076 --> 00:23:49.140
them of prion disease.

00:23:49.141 --> 00:23:50.310
So that's the expected number,

00:23:50.311 --> 00:23:52.285
the actual observed number
turns out to be much higher.

00:23:52.286 --> 00:23:54.160
So we found 16
different mutations.

00:23:54.161 --> 00:23:57.358
Sorry, sorry, I think it's 12
different mutations in PRNP that

00:23:57.359 --> 00:23:59.715
had previously been reported
to be pathogenic and

00:23:59.716 --> 00:24:01.300
52 individuals carried them.

00:24:01.301 --> 00:24:03.518
So that's 30 times higher than
what we would expect to see,

00:24:03.519 --> 00:24:05.810
given the frequency of
the disease in the population.

00:24:05.811 --> 00:24:07.290
There's multiple possible
explanations here.

00:24:07.291 --> 00:24:09.400
It could be that prion disease
is actually terrifyingly more

00:24:09.401 --> 00:24:10.830
common than we think it is.

00:24:10.831 --> 00:24:12.560
We're pretty confident
that's not the case.

00:24:12.561 --> 00:24:14.490
So by far, the most likely
explanation is that some of

00:24:14.491 --> 00:24:16.460
these previously
reported disease-causing

00:24:16.461 --> 00:24:18.710
mutations actually don't
cause disease in everyone.

00:24:18.711 --> 00:24:20.866
Or perhaps, in some cases,
don't cause disease in anyone.

00:24:20.867 --> 00:24:22.660
They've been falsely reported
as being pathogenic.

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:27.070
So to explore that, we generated
a plot that looks like this,

00:24:27.071 --> 00:24:28.420
and as well as a bunch of

00:24:28.421 --> 00:24:30.600
statistical analysis that I'm
not gonna go through here.

00:24:30.601 --> 00:24:33.361
This plot is very simple,
basically what is shows in

00:24:33.362 --> 00:24:36.437
the x-axis is the number of
times we observed a particular

00:24:36.438 --> 00:24:39.481
reported prion disease causing
a mutation in our cases.

00:24:39.482 --> 00:24:41.471
So that basically tells us
how common it is in patients

00:24:41.472 --> 00:24:43.620
who went on to get the disease.

00:24:43.621 --> 00:24:45.390
And on the y-axis we have
the frequency of the same

00:24:45.391 --> 00:24:46.780
variants in x-axis.

00:24:46.781 --> 00:24:49.760
Again, a set of population
controls broadly representative

00:24:49.761 --> 00:24:51.500
of the general population.

00:24:51.501 --> 00:24:53.810
And the variants then fall up
to three broad categories.

00:24:53.811 --> 00:24:58.415
Along the x-axis, we have
variants that are seen in cases,

00:24:58.416 --> 00:25:01.951
but are seen never or
very rarely in controls.

00:25:01.952 --> 00:25:04.775
These four variants along this
axis all turn out to be variants

00:25:04.776 --> 00:25:07.711
with incredibly strong evidence
for actually being pathogenic

00:25:07.712 --> 00:25:10.699
and disease-causing And as far
as we can tell, every individual

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:13.252
who has ever carried these
variants has indeed gone on to

00:25:13.253 --> 00:25:16.030
die from prion disease if they
survive into their fifties.

00:25:16.031 --> 00:25:18.120
And unfortunately that
does include D178N,

00:25:18.121 --> 00:25:19.460
which is the variant
that Sonia carries.

00:25:20.878 --> 00:25:24.250
On the y-axis, we have a set of
variants that are at relatively

00:25:24.251 --> 00:25:25.940
low frequency in cases.

00:25:25.941 --> 00:25:27.580
They're also at
relatively low frequency,

00:25:27.581 --> 00:25:29.530
basically the same
frequency in controls.

00:25:29.531 --> 00:25:32.020
So there's no information here
whatsoever to indicate that

00:25:32.021 --> 00:25:35.060
these variants are actually
associated with prion disease.

00:25:35.061 --> 00:25:37.470
And in fact certainly for these
variants that cluster up here,

00:25:37.471 --> 00:25:39.390
we can very confidently rule
them out as having any effect

00:25:39.391 --> 00:25:41.380
whatsoever on prion
disease risk at all.

00:25:41.381 --> 00:25:43.696
So these are variants that have
accidentally been associated

00:25:43.697 --> 00:25:45.611
with prion disease,
because they happened to be in

00:25:45.612 --> 00:25:47.748
an individual who died from
sporadic prion disease, and

00:25:47.749 --> 00:25:49.321
as a result they ended
up in the database.

00:25:49.322 --> 00:25:51.790
And we can now in most
cases throw those away.

00:25:51.791 --> 00:25:53.190
And then there's
a set of variants,

00:25:53.191 --> 00:25:54.370
really interesting
variants in the middle,

00:25:54.371 --> 00:25:56.890
who are too common in cases
to be completely benign, but

00:25:56.891 --> 00:25:59.660
also too common in controls
to be fully pathogenic.

00:25:59.661 --> 00:26:03.440
And these are variants that
clearly are a product of

00:26:03.441 --> 00:26:04.890
incomplete penetrance, that is,

00:26:04.891 --> 00:26:06.985
they cause diseases in some
people but not in others.

00:26:06.986 --> 00:26:10.740
And we can calculate with
some confidence the actual,

00:26:10.741 --> 00:26:12.560
absolute lifetime risk
of prion disease, for

00:26:12.561 --> 00:26:14.350
people who carry each
of these mutations.

00:26:14.351 --> 00:26:16.260
And that ranges enormously
from 1 in 1,000 risk for

00:26:16.261 --> 00:26:19.140
this variant up here, but
still confidently above 0, and

00:26:19.141 --> 00:26:22.550
down to about 10% risk for
this variant down here.

00:26:22.551 --> 00:26:24.760
That has profound implications
for genetic counseling.

00:26:24.761 --> 00:26:27.050
For all three of these variants,
there was information in the

00:26:27.051 --> 00:26:29.120
literature that suggested that
there was incomplete penetrance,

00:26:29.121 --> 00:26:32.270
but we had no idea what these
risk estimates actually were.

00:26:32.271 --> 00:26:35.220
And now people who are working
with families affected by these

00:26:35.221 --> 00:26:36.411
mutations can go back and

00:26:36.412 --> 00:26:39.021
give them a relatively well
calibrated estimate of how

00:26:39.022 --> 00:26:41.745
likely it is that another
family member who carries those

00:26:41.746 --> 00:26:43.696
mutations will go on
to get the disease.

00:26:43.697 --> 00:26:47.060
Just to give you an example
about how this information can

00:26:47.061 --> 00:26:47.972
change lives,

00:26:47.973 --> 00:26:51.689
there was an opinion piece that
accompanied the publication of

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:55.090
this prion disease work
back earlier last year.

00:26:55.091 --> 00:26:57.220
And in that, Robert Green,
a clinician at Brigham and

00:26:57.221 --> 00:26:59.163
Women's, describes a patient
that he was seeing at the time.

00:26:59.164 --> 00:27:02.637
This patient's mother had died,
like Sonia's mother had died,

00:27:02.638 --> 00:27:03.637
of prion disease.

00:27:03.638 --> 00:27:05.520
It was unknown what
the cause was.

00:27:05.521 --> 00:27:08.780
This patient had her
prion gene sequenced and

00:27:08.781 --> 00:27:12.225
was found to carry
a mutation called E196A.

00:27:12.226 --> 00:27:15.080
And she had been told as
a result that it was very likely

00:27:15.081 --> 00:27:17.944
she would go on to die from
prion disease in her 50s.

00:27:17.945 --> 00:27:20.939
It turns out that E196A is the
mutation for which we are by far

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:23.992
the most confident that this has
absolutely no association with

00:27:23.993 --> 00:27:25.010
prion disease.

00:27:25.011 --> 00:27:26.460
This is a completely
accidental finding.

00:27:26.461 --> 00:27:29.386
And this woman, Robert was able
to return that data back to her.

00:27:29.387 --> 00:27:30.967
He was also able to say that,
looking back

00:27:30.968 --> 00:27:32.956
through the literature,
there is no information on

00:27:32.957 --> 00:27:35.324
segregation of this variant
within prion disease families.

00:27:35.325 --> 00:27:36.769
There is no family
history in patients who

00:27:36.770 --> 00:27:37.934
carry that particular variant.

00:27:37.935 --> 00:27:40.598
And the frequency is absolutely
no higher in cases than it is in

00:27:40.599 --> 00:27:41.510
controls.

00:27:41.511 --> 00:27:43.747
So her probability of going
on to get prion disease is no

00:27:43.748 --> 00:27:45.315
higher than
the population average.

00:27:45.316 --> 00:27:48.124
So basically, Robert was able
to commute a death sentence in

00:27:48.125 --> 00:27:49.233
this particular case.

00:27:49.234 --> 00:27:50.025
And that, of course,

00:27:50.026 --> 00:27:52.000
can have transformative
impacts in other ways as well.

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:55.235
So this information did not
change, as far as we know,

00:27:55.236 --> 00:27:57.820
Sonia's risk of going on
to get prion disease.

00:27:57.821 --> 00:28:00.525
But I think it did, in many
ways, reestablish Eric and

00:28:00.526 --> 00:28:03.130
Sonia's commitment to working
as hard as they can to come up

00:28:03.131 --> 00:28:05.756
with a therapeutic approach to
reducing the levels of prion

00:28:05.757 --> 00:28:07.510
protein in Sonia's brain.

00:28:07.511 --> 00:28:10.714
To the level that it's actually
possible to ameliorate her risk

00:28:10.715 --> 00:28:12.384
of going on to get
prion disease.

00:28:12.385 --> 00:28:12.884
In this case,

00:28:12.885 --> 00:28:14.811
the therapeutic target is
actually extremely clear.

00:28:14.812 --> 00:28:17.091
If it's possible to get
a chemical agent into Sonia's

00:28:17.092 --> 00:28:19.766
brain that reduces the overall
levels of native prion protein,

00:28:19.767 --> 00:28:21.004
it's almost certain that she

00:28:21.005 --> 00:28:22.406
will be able to
reduce that risk.

00:28:22.407 --> 00:28:22.963
And Eric and

00:28:22.964 --> 00:28:25.474
Sonia are now working at
a lab at the Broad Institute.

00:28:25.475 --> 00:28:26.919
They're PhD students at Harvard,
but

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:29.430
amazingly have their own
lab at the Broad Institute.

00:28:29.431 --> 00:28:32.060
They're working in collaboration
with a number of industry

00:28:32.061 --> 00:28:34.689
partners to produce compounds
that will hopefully be able to

00:28:34.690 --> 00:28:37.626
produce exactly that effect over
the next 10 to 15 years, which

00:28:37.627 --> 00:28:40.279
is the time that Sonia has left
to come up with such a therapy.

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:42.590
And of course, we all wish
them the best in that quest.

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:47.430
So we have a lot of work to do
to be able to expand gnomAD to

00:28:47.431 --> 00:28:49.410
be able to answer
similar questions for

00:28:49.411 --> 00:28:51.430
a whole range of
other disease genes.

00:28:51.431 --> 00:28:54.320
We're very interested in
continuing to expand this.

00:28:54.321 --> 00:28:55.770
We will be expanding this to

00:28:55.771 --> 00:28:58.070
over 65,000 whole human
genomes with a new

00:28:58.071 --> 00:29:00.265
core set that will hopefully
be generated later this year,

00:29:00.266 --> 00:29:03.280
calling structural variants
across the entire dataset.

00:29:03.281 --> 00:29:05.655
And a set of analyses focused
on protein-truncating variants,

00:29:05.656 --> 00:29:08.310
as well as looking at constraint
in non-coding regions.

00:29:08.311 --> 00:29:10.686
And I think the key points
that it's worth emphasizing in

00:29:10.687 --> 00:29:13.311
the context of this presentation
is, firstly, the naturally

00:29:13.312 --> 00:29:15.639
occurring human genetic
variation is really useful for

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:18.413
understanding both the impact of
variation in a disease setting,

00:29:18.414 --> 00:29:20.570
but also in terms of
understanding human biology.

00:29:20.571 --> 00:29:22.485
That the more humans
we have sequenced and

00:29:22.486 --> 00:29:25.385
phenotyped, and the more diverse
their ancestry, the better

00:29:25.386 --> 00:29:27.968
off we are in terms of
understanding this disease risk.

00:29:27.969 --> 00:29:30.342
That everyone carries
rare genetic variants.

00:29:30.343 --> 00:29:31.920
And it's true that most
interesting variants are rare,

00:29:31.921 --> 00:29:34.110
but it's also importantly true
that most rare variants are not

00:29:34.111 --> 00:29:35.770
actually particularly
interesting.

00:29:35.771 --> 00:29:37.125
And these large scale
reference databases

00:29:37.126 --> 00:29:39.460
help to distinguish between
those two scenarios.

00:29:39.461 --> 00:29:41.390
And finally, one of the things
that I think has been a real

00:29:41.391 --> 00:29:43.123
strength of ExAc and
gnomAD has been,

00:29:43.124 --> 00:29:45.590
we've been very fortunate to
benefit from a human genomics

00:29:45.591 --> 00:29:48.520
community that's been willing to
share data openly and rapidly.

00:29:48.521 --> 00:29:50.403
But there's still a lot of work
that we as a community can

00:29:50.404 --> 00:29:51.496
do to make that process better.

00:29:51.497 --> 00:29:52.954
And it's really only
with that open and

00:29:52.955 --> 00:29:55.164
rapid data sharing that we can
build resources like this and

00:29:55.165 --> 00:29:56.460
make them available
to the world.

00:29:57.520 --> 00:29:58.140
So with that,

00:29:58.141 --> 00:30:00.070
I'll finish by thanking
everyone who's been involved in

00:30:00.071 --> 00:30:03.200
generating and analyzing
this important resource.

00:30:03.201 --> 00:30:06.175
Again, including the analysis
team, production team at Broad,

00:30:06.176 --> 00:30:09.043
the individuals who built the
gnomAD website, and once again,

00:30:09.044 --> 00:30:10.953
the genomics platform and
the Hail team for

00:30:10.954 --> 00:30:13.260
all of the critical work
in building this data out.

00:30:13.261 --> 00:30:14.574
Thanks to you guys for
listening.

00:30:14.575 --> 00:30:19.390
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

